1
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,516
There are places on Earth where
the land itself seems to forget.

2
00:00:10,966 --> 00:00:15,834
Here, in the south of Arabia, the wind
has been moving sand for thousands of

3
00:00:15,874 --> 00:00:16,672
years.

4
00:00:16,712 --> 00:00:17,712
Lifting it.

5
00:00:18,102 --> 00:00:19,102
Folding it.

6
00:00:19,429 --> 00:00:20,900
Laying it down again.

7
00:00:21,350 --> 00:00:23,182
Footprints vanish by evening.

8
00:00:23,632 --> 00:00:25,398
Whole dunes walk.

9
00:00:28,498 --> 00:00:32,015
And yet, some names survive the wind.

10
00:00:32,465 --> 00:00:34,538
One of them is Iram.

11
00:00:43,138 --> 00:00:47,347
The Rubʿ al-Khālī, the Empty Quarter,
covers some six hundred and fifty

12
00:00:47,387 --> 00:00:51,775
thousand square kilometres of the
Arabian Peninsula, across parts of Saudi

13
00:00:51,815 --> 00:00:54,747
Arabia, Oman, the United
Arab Emirates and Yemen.

14
00:00:55,197 --> 00:00:59,409
It is often described as the largest
continuous sand desert on Earth.

15
00:00:59,859 --> 00:01:03,550
In places, its dunes rise
more than two hundred metres.

16
00:01:04,150 --> 00:01:08,870
For centuries, those who lived along
its edges told stories of a people of

17
00:01:08,910 --> 00:01:13,220
great strength, of towering buildings,
and of a city that was lost.

18
00:01:13,820 --> 00:01:16,878
The oldest written source
for that memory is the Quran.

19
00:01:17,328 --> 00:01:22,888
In a short passage of Sūrat al-Fajr,
the chapter of the Dawn, it asks:

20
00:01:23,488 --> 00:01:27,931
"Dost thou not consider how thy Lord
dealt with the tribe of ʿĀd, with

21
00:01:27,971 --> 00:01:32,326
many-columned Iram, the like of
which was not created in the lands."

22
00:01:32,926 --> 00:01:34,685
Three short verses.

23
00:01:35,135 --> 00:01:37,111
A people: ʿĀd.

24
00:01:37,561 --> 00:01:39,733
A name: Iram.

25
00:01:40,183 --> 00:01:43,231
And a description: dhāt al-ʿimād.

26
00:01:43,681 --> 00:01:44,958
Possessing ʿimād.

27
00:01:45,408 --> 00:01:49,788
A word that can mean columns, or pillars,
or the poles that hold up a tent.

28
00:01:50,388 --> 00:01:55,024
From these few words grew one of the
most enduring mysteries of the Arabian

29
00:01:55,064 --> 00:01:55,910
desert.

30
00:01:55,950 --> 00:01:58,663
Storytellers imagined palaces of gold.

31
00:01:59,113 --> 00:02:02,793
Popular retellings still speak
of a city of a thousand pillars.

32
00:02:03,243 --> 00:02:05,155
Explorers went looking for it.

33
00:02:05,605 --> 00:02:10,395
And satellites photographed
the sand where it might lie.

34
00:02:10,995 --> 00:02:13,279
This film asks three questions.

35
00:02:13,729 --> 00:02:16,242
What does the text actually say?

36
00:02:16,805 --> 00:02:19,725
What did the earliest
scholars understand by it?

37
00:02:20,287 --> 00:02:23,769
And what, if anything, has
archaeology really found?

38
00:02:24,369 --> 00:02:29,609
To answer them, we must keep different
kinds of evidence apart: scripture,

39
00:02:29,649 --> 00:02:33,003
scholarship, legend,
excavation and hypothesis.

40
00:02:33,453 --> 00:02:37,161
Throughout this film, each
will be marked as it appears.

41
00:02:44,761 --> 00:02:48,162
The Quran names ʿĀd twenty-four times.

42
00:02:48,612 --> 00:02:51,843
Iram, it names only once.

43
00:02:52,443 --> 00:02:56,744
In the Quranic account, ʿĀd were a
people who came after the people of Noah.

44
00:02:57,194 --> 00:02:59,745
To them was sent a prophet
from among themselves.

45
00:03:00,195 --> 00:03:02,984
The Quran calls him their brother: Hūd.

46
00:03:03,584 --> 00:03:06,898
It describes a people blessed with plenty.

47
00:03:07,348 --> 00:03:11,514
Hūd reminds them that God has aided
them "with cattle and sons, and gardens

48
00:03:11,554 --> 00:03:12,564
and watersprings."

49
00:03:13,014 --> 00:03:17,276
He reminds them that they were given
"growth of stature": in Arabic, basṭa,

50
00:03:17,316 --> 00:03:20,757
a word the commentators read
as size, or strength, or reach.

51
00:03:21,357 --> 00:03:23,583
They were builders.

52
00:03:24,033 --> 00:03:28,016
"Build ye on every high place
a monument for vain delight?"

53
00:03:28,579 --> 00:03:29,796
Hūd asks them.

54
00:03:30,246 --> 00:03:34,531
"And seek ye out strongholds,
that haply ye may last for ever?"

55
00:03:35,131 --> 00:03:37,356
And they were certain of their own power.

56
00:03:37,806 --> 00:03:40,260
"Who is mightier than us in power?"

57
00:03:40,822 --> 00:03:41,822
they ask.

58
00:03:42,332 --> 00:03:45,727
The Quran tells us where Hūd
preached in a single phrase.

59
00:03:46,177 --> 00:03:52,492
He warned his people, it says, "among
the wind-curved sandhills": bil-Aḥqāf.

60
00:03:53,092 --> 00:03:56,343
Classical scholars tried to
find that landscape on the map.

61
00:03:56,793 --> 00:04:01,217
The great exegete al-Ṭabarī, who died
in Baghdad in the year nine hundred

62
00:04:01,257 --> 00:04:05,659
and twenty-three, explained that aḥqāf
means sand that curves and bends.

63
00:04:06,109 --> 00:04:11,684
He placed the land of ʿĀd in the
south: in al-Shiḥr, in the region of

64
00:04:11,724 --> 00:04:14,572
Ḥaḍramawt, and the lands around it.

65
00:04:15,172 --> 00:04:20,121
In Ḥaḍramawt today, a tomb attributed
to the Prophet Hūd is still visited by

66
00:04:20,161 --> 00:04:21,161
pilgrims.

67
00:04:21,202 --> 00:04:25,065
It is a living tradition, though more
than one place has claimed his resting

68
00:04:25,105 --> 00:04:25,973
place.

69
00:04:26,013 --> 00:04:31,726
What the Quran does not give us is
a date, a map, or a description of a

70
00:04:31,766 --> 00:04:32,581
city.

71
00:04:32,621 --> 00:04:37,532
It gives a moral history: a people
given strength and plenty; a warning; a

72
00:04:37,572 --> 00:04:38,572
refusal.

73
00:04:38,707 --> 00:04:41,696
In this film, the Prophet
Hūd is never shown.

74
00:04:42,146 --> 00:04:46,541
And the people of ʿĀd appear only as
distant figures, because no one knows

75
00:04:46,581 --> 00:04:47,900
what they looked like.

76
00:04:55,500 --> 00:04:58,161
Iram dhāt al-ʿimād.

77
00:04:58,611 --> 00:05:01,295
Translators still disagree
on how to render it.

78
00:05:01,745 --> 00:05:06,800
Marmaduke Pickthall, in nineteen
thirty, wrote "many-columned Iram".

79
00:05:07,250 --> 00:05:12,188
A modern translation, Saheeh
International, has "Iram, who had lofty

80
00:05:12,228 --> 00:05:13,228
pillars".

81
00:05:13,337 --> 00:05:15,376
Even the next verse divides them.

82
00:05:15,826 --> 00:05:19,876
Was Iram a place, "the like
of which" was never created?

83
00:05:20,438 --> 00:05:22,959
Or a people, "the likes of whom"?

84
00:05:23,559 --> 00:05:27,561
Muslim scholars were asking the same
questions more than a thousand years

85
00:05:27,601 --> 00:05:28,382
ago.

86
00:05:28,422 --> 00:05:31,777
Al-Ṭabarī gathered the opinions
of the generations before him.

87
00:05:32,227 --> 00:05:37,637
Some said Iram was a city: Alexandria,
according to one early authority;

88
00:05:37,677 --> 00:05:40,024
Damascus, according to another.

89
00:05:40,474 --> 00:05:42,095
Some said it was a nation.

90
00:05:42,545 --> 00:05:47,449
Others said it was a clan of ʿĀd, "the
royal house of ʿĀd", in the words of

91
00:05:47,489 --> 00:05:51,180
the early scholar Qatāda, or
the name of their ancestor.

92
00:05:51,780 --> 00:05:56,709
Al-Ṭabarī weighed these views, and
concluded that Iram was most likely the

93
00:05:56,749 --> 00:05:58,294
name of a tribe of ʿĀd.

94
00:05:58,744 --> 00:06:00,895
Then he turned to the pillars.

95
00:06:01,495 --> 00:06:07,269
Some early authorities said the word
described the people themselves: their

96
00:06:07,309 --> 00:06:08,317
great height.

97
00:06:08,767 --> 00:06:13,407
One said it meant a building
they had raised, on tall columns.

98
00:06:13,857 --> 00:06:16,457
But the view al-Ṭabarī
preferred was simpler.

99
00:06:16,907 --> 00:06:20,417
The ʿimād, he argued,
were the poles of tents.

100
00:06:20,867 --> 00:06:27,334
ʿĀd were "people of poles": travellers,
moving with the rains and the

101
00:06:27,374 --> 00:06:28,374
pasture.

102
00:06:28,728 --> 00:06:32,336
And he added a sentence that cuts
to the heart of this mystery.

103
00:06:33,036 --> 00:06:38,316
ولا يعلم بناء كان لهم بالعماد بخبر صحيح

104
00:06:39,016 --> 00:06:43,404
No building of theirs with columns, he
wrote, is known through any authentic

105
00:06:43,444 --> 00:06:44,444
report.

106
00:06:45,952 --> 00:06:51,048
Four centuries later, in Damascus,
the scholar Ibn Kathīr agreed.

107
00:06:51,748 --> 00:06:54,308
فهم قبيلة وأمة من الأمم

108
00:06:55,008 --> 00:07:00,754
Whether the ʿimād were buildings, or
tent poles, or weapons, or the height

109
00:07:00,794 --> 00:07:06,424
of the people, he wrote, ʿĀd were a
tribe: "a nation among the nations".

110
00:07:07,024 --> 00:07:09,228
So where did the golden city come from?

111
00:07:09,791 --> 00:07:13,692
By Ibn Kathīr's time, a rich
legend had grown around the verse.

112
00:07:14,142 --> 00:07:19,331
In some versions, a king named
Shaddād, son of ʿĀd, built it.

113
00:07:19,781 --> 00:07:24,607
Commentators described a city of
bricks of gold and silver, its pebbles

114
00:07:24,647 --> 00:07:27,457
pearls and jewels, its soil made of musk.

115
00:07:27,907 --> 00:07:33,580
A city that moved from land to land:
sometimes in Syria, sometimes in Yemen,

116
00:07:33,620 --> 00:07:34,973
sometimes in Iraq.

117
00:07:35,573 --> 00:07:40,090
One story told of a Bedouin, ʿAbd Allāh
ibn Qilāba, who, while searching for

118
00:07:40,130 --> 00:07:44,528
his lost camels in the time of the
caliph Muʿāwiya, came upon a great city

119
00:07:44,568 --> 00:07:45,827
with walls and gates.

120
00:07:46,277 --> 00:07:49,791
When others went back with him
to the place, they found nothing.

121
00:07:50,391 --> 00:07:53,129
Ibn Kathīr rejected all of it.

122
00:07:53,829 --> 00:08:01,639
فإن هذا كله من خرافات الإسرائيليين.
فهذه الحكاية ليس يصح إسنادها.

123
00:08:02,339 --> 00:08:05,830
These were, in his words,
fables of the Israʾīliyyāt.

124
00:08:06,280 --> 00:08:11,479
The tale of Ibn Qilāba, he said, had
no sound chain of transmission, and was

125
00:08:11,519 --> 00:08:13,173
certainly not authentic.

126
00:08:13,773 --> 00:08:15,963
The legend lived on regardless.

127
00:08:16,413 --> 00:08:20,820
It entered the Thousand and One Nights,
and later the poetry and fiction of

128
00:08:20,860 --> 00:08:21,835
Europe.

129
00:08:21,875 --> 00:08:25,727
What you are seeing now is
an artistic reconstruction.

130
00:08:26,177 --> 00:08:32,360
One imagined reading of the verse: a
settlement raised on stone columns.

131
00:08:32,810 --> 00:08:35,425
It is not based on any excavated site.

132
00:08:35,875 --> 00:08:41,667
The Quran does not describe Iram's
appearance, and no archaeologist has

133
00:08:41,707 --> 00:08:42,707
found it.

134
00:08:43,046 --> 00:08:47,814
And this is the other reading, the
one the classical scholars preferred:

135
00:08:47,854 --> 00:08:52,329
great tents of woven hair, raised
on poles, moving across the land.

136
00:08:52,779 --> 00:08:54,956
It too is an interpretation.

137
00:08:55,406 --> 00:08:58,637
But it reminds us how much
a single word can carry.

138
00:08:59,237 --> 00:09:03,172
The city of a thousand
pillars is real, in its way.

139
00:09:03,622 --> 00:09:06,462
But it belongs to the
history of storytelling.

140
00:09:06,912 --> 00:09:09,819
The text itself is far more restrained.

141
00:09:17,919 --> 00:09:22,604
In the twentieth century, the search
moved from the library into the desert.

142
00:09:23,204 --> 00:09:28,320
In nineteen thirty, the British explorer
Bertram Thomas set out to cross the

143
00:09:28,360 --> 00:09:31,956
Empty Quarter, guided by
Bedouin of the Rashīd tribe.

144
00:09:32,406 --> 00:09:35,562
He became the first
Westerner on record to do so.

145
00:09:36,012 --> 00:09:40,528
Along the way, his guides pointed
to wide tracks between the dunes.

146
00:09:40,978 --> 00:09:43,126
"Look, Sahib," one said.

147
00:09:43,576 --> 00:09:45,036
"There is the way to Ubar."

148
00:09:45,636 --> 00:09:48,259
Thomas wrote of "this
Atlantis of the Sands".

149
00:09:48,709 --> 00:09:52,935
The phrase is often credited to Lawrence
of Arabia, but it does not appear

150
00:09:52,975 --> 00:09:54,705
in Lawrence's published works.

151
00:09:55,305 --> 00:10:01,337
Ubar, or Wabār, was a name from Arabian
tradition: a fabled place of wealth

152
00:10:01,377 --> 00:10:03,968
and ruin somewhere in the sands.

153
00:10:04,418 --> 00:10:08,373
Over time, some writers began to
merge it with the Quranic Iram.

154
00:10:08,973 --> 00:10:13,986
In nineteen thirty-two, Harry St John
Philby went in search of Wabār, deep

155
00:10:14,026 --> 00:10:15,460
in the Rubʿ al-Khālī.

156
00:10:15,910 --> 00:10:21,533
Bedouin had told him of ruins, and of
a block of iron the size of a camel.

157
00:10:21,983 --> 00:10:26,408
After a month of hard travel, he reached
a field of black glass and craters

158
00:10:26,448 --> 00:10:27,758
half choked with sand.

159
00:10:28,208 --> 00:10:30,028
He thought it was a volcano.

160
00:10:30,628 --> 00:10:33,149
It was neither a city nor a volcano.

161
00:10:33,599 --> 00:10:37,979
Samples sent to the British Museum
showed that it was the impact site of an

162
00:10:38,019 --> 00:10:39,019
iron meteorite.

163
00:10:39,353 --> 00:10:43,101
Later luminescence dating suggests the
craters may be only a few centuries

164
00:10:43,141 --> 00:10:45,751
old, perhaps less than two
hundred and fifty years.

165
00:10:46,201 --> 00:10:49,126
A real place, and a real mystery.

166
00:10:49,576 --> 00:10:51,242
But not Iram.

167
00:10:53,342 --> 00:10:58,338
To understand why anyone would travel
through this desert at all, follow the

168
00:10:58,378 --> 00:10:59,836
smoke of frankincense.

169
00:11:00,286 --> 00:11:05,464
Frankincense is the resin of Boswellia
sacra, a tree that still grows in the

170
00:11:05,504 --> 00:11:08,250
dry valleys of Dhofar, in southern Oman.

171
00:11:08,700 --> 00:11:12,087
In the ancient world, it was
prized from Arabia to Rome.

172
00:11:12,687 --> 00:11:16,722
A Greek sailing guide of the first
century, the Periplus of the Erythraean

173
00:11:16,762 --> 00:11:20,633
Sea, describes this coast as "the
Frankincense Country, mountainous and

174
00:11:20,673 --> 00:11:23,151
forbidding, wrapped in
thick clouds and fog."

175
00:11:23,601 --> 00:11:27,916
It names a harbour called Moscha, where
ships took on the resin, under the

176
00:11:27,956 --> 00:11:29,780
control of the king's officers.

177
00:11:30,380 --> 00:11:35,540
Moscha is identified with Khor Rori,
near modern Salalah, where the walls of

178
00:11:35,580 --> 00:11:38,659
a fortified port called
Sumhuram still stand.

179
00:11:39,109 --> 00:11:43,400
From this coast and its hinterland,
frankincense moved inland and north,

180
00:11:43,440 --> 00:11:48,011
along caravan routes that linked southern
Arabia to the Mediterranean world.

181
00:11:48,611 --> 00:11:53,346
Decades after Thomas, an American
filmmaker, Nicholas Clapp, read his

182
00:11:53,386 --> 00:11:55,670
account and set out to find Ubar.

183
00:11:56,120 --> 00:12:00,720
He brought together scientists from
NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory, among

184
00:12:00,760 --> 00:12:05,236
them Ronald Blom and Charles Elachi,
with the archaeologist Juris Zarins,

185
00:12:05,276 --> 00:12:08,245
the explorer Ranulph
Fiennes, and George Hedges.

186
00:12:08,845 --> 00:12:11,119
Satellites could not see a buried city.

187
00:12:11,569 --> 00:12:16,428
But Landsat images revealed something
subtler: faint, ancient tracks

188
00:12:16,468 --> 00:12:18,341
crossing the desert floor.

189
00:12:18,791 --> 00:12:21,216
And several of them led towards one place.

190
00:12:21,666 --> 00:12:26,428
In nineteen ninety-four, radar aboard
the Space Shuttle Endeavour imaged the

191
00:12:26,468 --> 00:12:30,512
same region, and the tracks
appeared again, as soft red streaks.

192
00:12:31,112 --> 00:12:35,617
That place was Shisr, a small oasis in
Dhofar, about one hundred and seventy

193
00:12:35,657 --> 00:12:39,743
kilometres inland, with a Bedouin
well and a ruined fort that earlier

194
00:12:39,783 --> 00:12:42,952
travellers had dismissed as
only a few centuries old.

195
00:12:43,552 --> 00:12:46,201
Excavation began under Juris Zarins.

196
00:12:46,651 --> 00:12:51,406
Beneath the later fort, the team
uncovered the walls and towers of a much

197
00:12:51,446 --> 00:12:56,136
older fortress, more than two thousand
years old in its earliest phases,

198
00:12:56,176 --> 00:12:58,015
according to its excavators.

199
00:12:58,465 --> 00:13:03,348
They found objects from Persia, Greece
and Rome: the traces of long-distance

200
00:13:03,388 --> 00:13:04,187
trade.

201
00:13:04,227 --> 00:13:08,221
And they found that much of the fortress
had collapsed into a sinkhole, the

202
00:13:08,261 --> 00:13:10,197
limestone cavern that held the well.

203
00:13:10,797 --> 00:13:15,246
In February nineteen ninety-two,
newspapers around the world announced that

204
00:13:15,286 --> 00:13:17,500
the lost city of Ubar had been found.

205
00:13:19,600 --> 00:13:23,637
But the scientists themselves
soon told a more careful story.

206
00:13:24,087 --> 00:13:28,852
Juris Zarins argued that, in the
classical and Arabic sources, Ubar was not

207
00:13:28,892 --> 00:13:33,529
a single town at all, but a region
and a people: the Iobaritae, marked on

208
00:13:33,569 --> 00:13:35,747
the second-century map of Ptolemy.

209
00:13:36,197 --> 00:13:40,779
Shisr, in this view, was one fortified
station in that land, a vital source

210
00:13:40,819 --> 00:13:43,037
of water on the frankincense routes.

211
00:13:43,637 --> 00:13:45,047
Others went further.

212
00:13:45,497 --> 00:13:49,026
In two thousand and four, the geologist H.

213
00:13:49,476 --> 00:13:54,049
Stewart Edgell published a critique
titled "The myth of the lost city of the

214
00:13:54,089 --> 00:13:58,662
Arabian Sands", arguing that Shisr had
been a small caravanserai, not a lost

215
00:13:58,702 --> 00:13:59,702
metropolis.

216
00:13:59,970 --> 00:14:04,288
Today, Shisr is part of a UNESCO
World Heritage property, the Land of

217
00:14:04,328 --> 00:14:05,328
Frankincense.

218
00:14:05,599 --> 00:14:11,354
UNESCO describes it not as Iram, but
as "the remains of the caravan oasis of

219
00:14:11,394 --> 00:14:16,961
Shisr, or Wubar": a station for water
on the routes of the incense trade.

220
00:14:17,561 --> 00:14:19,670
So what did Shisr prove?

221
00:14:20,233 --> 00:14:23,876
It proved that ancient tracks
can be traced from space.

222
00:14:24,326 --> 00:14:27,901
It proved that this desert
edge was busy with trade.

223
00:14:28,351 --> 00:14:30,465
It did not prove that Shisr was Ubar.

224
00:14:30,915 --> 00:14:33,810
And it did not prove that Ubar was Iram.

225
00:14:34,410 --> 00:14:36,205
The difference matters.

226
00:14:36,655 --> 00:14:40,693
A popular identification is a
story that people choose to tell.

227
00:14:41,143 --> 00:14:44,854
An archaeological interpretation
is an argument from evidence.

228
00:14:45,304 --> 00:14:49,051
An established conclusion is what
remains when the arguments have been

229
00:14:49,091 --> 00:14:49,880
tested.

230
00:14:49,920 --> 00:14:54,582
The link between Shisr and Iram
has never moved beyond the first.

231
00:15:02,682 --> 00:15:07,056
The Quran's account of how ʿĀd
came to an end is brief, and vivid.

232
00:15:07,506 --> 00:15:09,721
They demanded the punishment
they had been warned of.

233
00:15:10,171 --> 00:15:11,803
And then, the text says,

234
00:15:12,403 --> 00:15:17,155
"when they beheld it as a dense cloud
coming toward their valleys, they

235
00:15:17,195 --> 00:15:19,827
said: Here is a cloud bringing us rain.

236
00:15:20,277 --> 00:15:26,110
Nay, but it is that which ye did seek
to hasten, a wind wherein is painful

237
00:15:26,150 --> 00:15:30,753
torment, destroying all things
by commandment of its Lord.

238
00:15:31,203 --> 00:15:34,947
And morning found them so that naught
could be seen save their dwellings."

239
00:15:37,047 --> 00:15:43,325
Elsewhere the wind is called ṣarṣar,
raging, or roaring, and al-ʿaqīm, the

240
00:15:43,365 --> 00:15:44,390
barren wind.

241
00:15:44,840 --> 00:15:49,188
It blew, the Quran says, "for seven
long nights and eight long days".

242
00:15:49,638 --> 00:15:53,435
Hūd, and those who believed
with him, were saved.

243
00:15:54,035 --> 00:15:57,155
The memory of that wind stayed
with the Prophet Muhammad.

244
00:15:57,605 --> 00:16:02,662
His wife ʿĀʾisha reported that
whenever he saw clouds or wind, concern

245
00:16:02,702 --> 00:16:04,085
showed on his face.

246
00:16:04,535 --> 00:16:08,570
When she asked him why, he said that
a people had been punished with wind,

247
00:16:08,610 --> 00:16:12,591
and that when they saw it coming, they
had said, "This cloud will give us

248
00:16:12,631 --> 00:16:13,521
rain."

249
00:16:13,561 --> 00:16:17,761
نُصِرْتُ بِالصَّبَا،
وَأُهْلِكَتْ عَادٌ بِالدَّبُورِ

250
00:16:18,461 --> 00:16:23,439
In another authentic report, he said:
"I was granted victory with the east

251
00:16:23,479 --> 00:16:26,666
wind, and ʿĀd were
destroyed by the west wind."

252
00:16:29,766 --> 00:16:33,977
Science cannot test a religious
account of divine judgement.

253
00:16:34,427 --> 00:16:37,963
But it can ask a different question:
how has this land itself changed?

254
00:16:38,563 --> 00:16:43,349
In a cave called Qunf, in the mountains
of Dhofar, a stalagmite has recorded

255
00:16:43,389 --> 00:16:46,437
rainfall for thousands
of years, layer by layer.

256
00:16:46,887 --> 00:16:51,220
A study published in two thousand and
three found that monsoon rain here was

257
00:16:51,260 --> 00:16:55,420
stronger in the early Holocene, and
that after about eight thousand years

258
00:16:55,460 --> 00:16:57,014
ago, it gradually declined.

259
00:16:57,614 --> 00:17:01,210
Deep in the Rubʿ al-Khālī, researchers
reported in two thousand and

260
00:17:01,250 --> 00:17:05,227
twenty-five that, during an ancient
humid phase, heavy rains filled a lake

261
00:17:05,267 --> 00:17:09,298
of about eleven hundred square kilometres,
which eventually broke its banks

262
00:17:09,338 --> 00:17:12,757
and carved a valley some one
hundred and fifty kilometres long.

263
00:17:13,357 --> 00:17:17,021
Arabia, in other words,
was not always this dry.

264
00:17:17,471 --> 00:17:22,656
Over many thousands of years, its
landscapes have shifted between green and

265
00:17:22,696 --> 00:17:23,604
gold.

266
00:17:23,644 --> 00:17:24,976
But we should be clear.

267
00:17:25,426 --> 00:17:29,051
These changes unfolded over
centuries and millennia.

268
00:17:29,501 --> 00:17:34,803
No study has dated the people of ʿĀd,
and no geological event has been shown

269
00:17:34,843 --> 00:17:36,881
to match the Quranic account.

270
00:17:37,331 --> 00:17:41,950
At Shisr, the excavators found no
evidence that a sandstorm destroyed the

271
00:17:41,990 --> 00:17:42,719
site.

272
00:17:42,759 --> 00:17:47,837
The collapse of its fortress into a
sinkhole has a geological explanation,

273
00:17:47,877 --> 00:17:50,021
and no known connection to ʿĀd.

274
00:17:50,621 --> 00:17:55,400
Religious memory and physical evidence
are answering different questions.

275
00:17:55,850 --> 00:17:58,976
Merging them would do justice to neither.

276
00:18:07,076 --> 00:18:11,774
In recent years, the search for Iram
has taken an unexpected turn, away from

277
00:18:11,814 --> 00:18:13,684
the southern sands altogether.

278
00:18:14,284 --> 00:18:20,030
In the deserts of southern Jordan lies
Wadi Ramm, the valley known today as

279
00:18:20,070 --> 00:18:25,394
Wadi Rum: red cliffs, and some twenty
thousand recorded inscriptions.

280
00:18:25,844 --> 00:18:30,691
A Nabataean temple to the goddess
Allat stands at the foot of Jabal Ramm,

281
00:18:30,731 --> 00:18:35,684
and inscriptions from the site give
the ancient name of the place as Iram.

282
00:18:36,284 --> 00:18:41,075
In two thousand and twenty-five, the
epigrapher Ahmad Al-Jallad published a

283
00:18:41,115 --> 00:18:45,905
study of pre-Islamic inscriptions
written by members of a tribe called ʿĀd,

284
00:18:45,945 --> 00:18:50,800
in the deserts of the north, and argued
that the Quranic names may belong to

285
00:18:50,840 --> 00:18:51,840
this region.

286
00:18:52,213 --> 00:18:57,284
It is a serious proposal, built on
careful readings of ancient texts.

287
00:18:57,734 --> 00:19:01,586
It is also new, and scholars
are still weighing it.

288
00:19:02,036 --> 00:19:06,944
Like Shisr before it, it shows how much
depends on the evidence we choose to

289
00:19:06,984 --> 00:19:07,984
follow.

290
00:19:10,040 --> 00:19:12,564
So, what can we actually say?

291
00:19:13,164 --> 00:19:18,196
The Quran tells of a people named
ʿĀd; of their prophet, Hūd; of their

292
00:19:18,236 --> 00:19:23,598
strength and their building; and of a
wind that left only their dwellings.

293
00:19:24,048 --> 00:19:28,276
It names Iram once, and
calls it dhāt al-ʿimād.

294
00:19:28,876 --> 00:19:31,467
The classical scholars recorded a debate.

295
00:19:31,917 --> 00:19:37,985
Two of the most influential, al-Ṭabarī
and Ibn Kathīr, understood Iram as a

296
00:19:38,025 --> 00:19:41,527
tribe, and its ʿimād
as the poles of tents.

297
00:19:41,977 --> 00:19:44,586
And they rejected the
legend of a golden city.

298
00:19:45,186 --> 00:19:50,740
Archaeology has found real places: a
caravan fortress at Shisr, frankincense

299
00:19:50,780 --> 00:19:56,260
ports on the coast, and, in the north,
inscriptions that name both Iram and

300
00:19:56,300 --> 00:19:57,699
a tribe called ʿĀd.

301
00:19:58,149 --> 00:20:01,629
It has not found a city
of a thousand pillars.

302
00:20:02,229 --> 00:20:08,359
And some things remain unknown: when
ʿĀd lived; exactly where; and what

303
00:20:08,399 --> 00:20:10,659
their pillars really were.

304
00:20:13,759 --> 00:20:17,043
Perhaps that is the real lesson of Iram.

305
00:20:17,493 --> 00:20:22,421
A people can survive in memory long
after their stones are gone, or even if

306
00:20:22,461 --> 00:20:24,647
they never built in stone at all.

307
00:20:25,097 --> 00:20:30,063
A few words in a sacred text have
outlived empires, maps and explorers.

308
00:20:30,663 --> 00:20:35,847
The wind still moves across the sand,
as it always has, erasing footprints

309
00:20:35,887 --> 00:20:36,887
by evening.

310
00:20:37,113 --> 00:20:39,577
What remains is the question.

311
00:20:40,027 --> 00:20:44,046
And the discipline to tell the
difference between what we know, what we

312
00:20:44,086 --> 00:20:46,030
believe, and what we only imagine.

